\chapter{The Pipeline Feature}
\label{ch:pipeline}

\section{Why a pipeline}

Most data processing in a domain-specific language is a sequence of
transformations applied to a value. A lexer turns characters into tokens; a
type checker turns tokens into a decorated syntax tree; a back end turns a
syntax tree into code. Written as nested function calls, such a sequence
reads inside out. The innermost call is the first transformation, and the
reader has to count parentheses to discover the order in which the
transformations happen. A pipeline reverses the presentation: the value comes
first and the transformations follow it in the order they are applied, so the
code reads in the same order as the computation.

\DSLtk{} provides that reading with three small pieces. A \emph{stage} is a
callable wrapped in a type that says ``this is a step in a pipeline''. A
factory function, \api{dsl::pipe}, produces such a stage from an ordinary
lambda. And a single overloaded \code{operator|} applies a stage to
whatever is on its left. The whole facility is twenty lines of code, but it
is the difference between a transformation chain that reads top to bottom
and one that reads bottom to top.

\section{The data model}

The facility consists of three entities. They are declared at lines
6064--6090 of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, immediately before the feature tag.

The first is the stage type \api{dsl::PipeStage<F>}, a struct with one
public member:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename F> struct PipeStage
{
  F fn;
  explicit constexpr PipeStage (F f) : fn (std::move (f)) {}
};
\end{lstlisting}

The stage holds the callable \code{fn} \emph{by value}. The constructor is
\code{constexpr} and \code{explicit}; the explicitness matters because the
factory returns a \code{PipeStage} directly, and it keeps a stage from
being produced by an accidental conversion.

The second is the factory:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename F>
constexpr auto
pipe (F &&f)
{
  return PipeStage<std::decay_t<F>>{ std::forward<F> (f) };
}
\end{lstlisting}

The factory decays its argument, so a stage built from a function lvalue,
a function reference or a temporary lambda all end up with the same
\code{PipeStage<F>} type. This matters more than it looks: a stage is a
\emph{type-level} object, and two stages compose only when their types are
compatible. Decay makes the type of a stage depend on what it does rather
than on how it was spelled at the call site.

The third entity is the operator itself:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename T, typename F>
constexpr auto
operator| (T &&val, PipeStage<F> stage)
{
  return stage.fn (std::forward<T> (val));
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{featurebox}[The operator is a free function]
\code{operator|} is declared at namespace scope inside \code{dsl}. It is
\emph{not} a member of the pipeline mixin, and it does not need to be found
by unqualified lookup: it is found by argument-dependent lookup, because
\code{PipeStage<F>} is a class type declared in namespace \code{dsl}. A
program therefore gets the operator for free, without inheriting from
anything and without a \code{using} declaration.
\end{featurebox}

This is a deliberate design choice and it has two consequences worth
making explicit. First, the operator works for any value type, including
types declared in the global namespace: because \code{dsl} is an associated
namespace of the \code{PipeStage} argument, the lookup succeeds no matter
where the left-hand operand was declared. Second, the feature tag
\code{dsl::Pipeline} is not what supplies the operator. It supplies
something else entirely, described next.

\section{The feature tag and its mixin}

The feature tag \api{dsl::Pipeline} (lines 6109--6125) has a mixin that
contains exactly one function:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename T>
static constexpr T
wrap (T &&v)
{
  return std::forward<T> (v);
}
\end{lstlisting}

\code{wrap} is the identity function. It exists so that a DSL class can be
the nominal starting point of a pipeline: \code{m.wrap(10) | stage} reads
as though the pipeline begins at the DSL object, even though the value
travels unchanged. Because \code{wrap} is static, it may be called either
through the class (\code{MyDSL::wrap(10)}) or through an instance
(\code{m.wrap(10)}); the latter is legal but stylistically odd, and the
class-qualified form is what a reader expects.

The mixin defines no operators, no member types and no state. A DSL that
inherits \code{dsl::Pipeline} gains precisely the ability to write
\code{MyDSL::wrap} and nothing else. The operator itself is available to
the whole program whether or not the feature tag is used at all.

\section{Evaluation order and desugaring}

\code{operator|} is left-associative in the \cpp{} grammar, so
\code{x | a | b} parses as \code{(x | a) | b}. The left operand of the
outer application is therefore the \emph{result} of the inner application,
not \code{x} itself. Since the inner application is
\code{a.fn(x)}, the outer application is \code{b.fn(a.fn(x))}.

\begin{pseudocode}[Desugaring \code{x | a | b}]
\textbf{expression} \code{x | a | b}\\[1ex]
\quad \textbf{group} as \code{(x | a) | b} \quad (left associativity)\\
\quad y \code{<-} \code{a.fn (x)}\\
\quad \textbf{return} \code{b.fn (y)}\\[1ex]
\quad \textbf{equivalently} \code{b.fn (a.fn (x))}
\end{pseudocode}

The important point is that the \emph{dataflow} is left to right even though
the \emph{nesting} is right to left. The value \code{x} enters the first
stage, the output of the first stage enters the second, and so on. The
reader sees the stages in the order they are applied. This is the same
\emph{dataflow} that the Haskell pipeline operator \code{|>} provides,
although the two differ in associativity: \code{|>} is right-associative,
so \code{x |> a |> b} is \code{b (a x)} with the stages written
right-to-left, whereas the \DSLtk{} form writes them left-to-right. The
\DSLtk{} form is the better match for \cpp{}, where \code{>} is not an
overloadable operator and where the left-to-right reading matches the order
in which the reader scans the line.

The stages are applied in sequence, and each stage is a plain function call.
There is no evaluation-order subtlety beyond the obvious one: the left
operand of each application is fully evaluated before the stage runs,
because it is an ordinary function argument.

\section{\code{|} versus \code{<<}}

It is natural to reach for the stream insertion operator, but the two are
not interchangeable.

\code{<<} is a member operator of the stream classes, and its left operand
is an ostream. It is left-associative, it is not a template, and it cannot
be overloaded for arbitrary types without inheriting from
\code{std::basic_ostream}. It also means \emph{output}, which is a
statement about a destination rather than about a transformation.

\code{|} in this facility means \emph{apply a transformation}. Its left
operand is any type whatsoever, and it is a template, so a stage can be
applied to a user-defined type with no inheritance and no adapter. The
result of one application is a value, and that value is what the next
application consumes; the pipeline is an expression, not a statement.

The two can of course be combined, and they compose naturally: the last
stage of a pipeline may produce something that is then streamed.

\section{Why the stage is taken by value}

The signature \code{operator|(T &&val, PipeStage<F> stage)} takes the
stage \emph{by value}. Three things follow, and all three are desirable.

First, a stage is reusable. Because the parameter is a copy rather than a
reference, the caller's stage is untouched:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
auto twice = dsl::pipe ([] (int x) { return x * 2; });
int a = 3 | twice;   // 6
int b = 4 | twice;   // 8
\end{lstlisting}

If the parameter were an rvalue reference, the first call would have
consumed the stage and the second would have operated on a moved-from
object.

Second, the pipeline is copyable. A \code{PipeStage<F>} is an ordinary
value type: it is copyable whenever \code{F} is copyable, and it can be
stored in a container, returned from a function, or captured by a lambda.
A user can therefore build a library of named stages and compose them.

Third, the interface is symmetric with the way the value is passed. The
value is forwarded as \code{T&&}, so a stage that takes its argument by
rvalue reference can move a temporary out of the pipeline, while a stage
that takes it by const reference sees it in place. The stage, by contrast,
is always copied, because the stage is the thing being \emph{used}, not
consumed.

\section{No allocation, no virtual dispatch}

Nothing in the facility allocates. \code{PipeStage<F>} stores its callable
inline, and \code{operator|} performs a direct call on it. There is no
heap, no type erasure, and no \code{std::function}. There is also no
virtual dispatch: \code{stage.fn(...)} is a static call whose target is
known at the point of instantiation, and the compiler will inline it.

The practical consequence is that a pipeline costs exactly what the stages
themselves cost. Adding stages does not add indirection; it adds the work
the stages do. This is a meaningful contrast with the general practice of
building transformation pipelines out of \code{std::function} objects, where
each stage typically costs one heap allocation and one indirect call.

\begin{featurebox}[The one caveat]
The no-allocation property holds only while \code{F} is a closure type that
stores its captures inline. A user who builds a stage from a
\code{std::function}, or from a lambda capturing a large object, gets
whatever allocation policy that callable has. The facility itself never
allocates.
\end{featurebox}

