The interaction worth dwelling on is with the parser. \code{dsl::Parser<T>}
has its own \code{operator|}, and it means ordered choice: the left parser
is tried first, and the right parser is tried only if the left one failed
without having committed to consuming input. That is a completely
different operation from the pipeline's, and yet both live in namespace
\code{dsl}. There is no conflict, because the parser overload requires
both operands to be \code{Parser<T>} for the same \code{T}, while the
pipeline overload requires its right operand to be a \code{PipeStage<F>}.
Neither deduction can succeed for the other's argument types, so overload
resolution never has to break a tie.

The same holds for the task pipeline. \code{dsl::operator|(Task, Task)}
builds a \code{TaskChain}, and \code{dsl::operator|(TaskChain, Task)}
extends one. These are exact, non-template matches, which outrank the
pipeline operator's template; and the pipeline operator cannot deduce
\code{F} from a \code{Task} in any case.

\begin{notebox}[The token is overloaded; the operation is not]
Whenever this book says ``the pipe operator'', it means the overload at
line 6087, the one that applies a stage. The parser's \code{|} is a
choice, the task pipeline's \code{|} is a concatenation, and the flag
operators' \code{|} is a bitwise union. The shared spelling is convenient
--- the operator a user reaches for first is the one that reads naturally
in the domain at hand --- but the meanings are unrelated.
\end{notebox}

\section{Two kinds of pipeline}

It is tempting to read the task pipeline of \chapref{ch:task-pipeline} as
another instance of the same idea, and the shared spelling of the operator
invites it. The two are related in metaphor but differ in kind, and
understanding how they differ is the fastest way to use both correctly.

A value pipeline is \emph{immediate}. The expression \code{x | s1 | s2}
evaluates the stages as it is evaluated: \code{s1} is applied to \code{x},
its result is applied to \code{s2}, and the whole thing is one expression
whose value is the last stage's output. Nothing is stored, nothing is
deferred, and the stages are concrete types known to the compiler.

A task pipeline is \emph{deferred}. The expression \code{t1 | t2} does not
run \code{t1}; it builds a \code{TaskChain} that holds both tasks. The work
happens later, when \code{dsl::run(state, chain)} walks the chain and
invokes each task with a shared \code{TaskState}. The chain accumulates
rather than nests: \code{t1 | t2 | t3} is a three-element vector, not a
tree of nested applications.

Three consequences follow from the difference. First, the ``value'' that
flows through a task pipeline is not the result of the previous task but
the mutable \code{TaskState}, which every task may read and write; the
results the tasks produce are collected in the state's results map. Second,
the task pipeline's stages are type-erased: a \code{Task} holds a
\code{std::function} of a fixed signature, so the chain presents a uniform
interface to the code that runs it while each task's body does whatever it
likes, at the cost of an indirect call and possibly a heap allocation. A
value pipeline's stages are concrete types and are called directly.
Third, a value pipeline produces a value, whereas a task pipeline produces
a side effect --- a mutated state --- and reports failure through the
\code{dsl::Result} that each task returns.

The same distinction reappears in the PEG combinators of
\chapref{ch:peg-combinators}, where \code{peg_seq} and \code{peg_choice}
compose parsers. There the composed object is again a value --- a new
parser --- and what flows through it is not a return value but the shared
input position, which the composed parsers advance in turn. In every case
the \code{|} operator means ``compose these two things'', and the question
of whether the composition is immediate or deferred is answered by the
types involved.

\section{Building a stage library}

Because a stage is an ordinary value with an ordinary type, a DSL can
publish a library of named stages and let its users compose them. The
pattern that the worked example follows is the useful one: declare the
stages as \code{static constexpr} members of the DSL class.

Three properties make this more than a convenience. The stages are
constructed at compile time, so there is no run-time setup cost. Their
types are distinct, so a pipeline whose stages are in the wrong order
fails to compile rather than misbehaving. And they are ordinary members, so
a user can find them by reading the class, pass them to a function, or
store them.

The type discipline is the part that pays off. Consider a DSL for text
processing in which one stage produces a \code{Token} and the next
consumes a \code{Token}. The following fragment is schematic --- the
stage names stand for members of some DSL class --- but the typing is
exactly what a real program would have:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
auto tokens   = source | dsl::pipe (lex);
auto lowered  = tokens | dsl::pipe (fold_case);
auto rendered = lowered | dsl::pipe (emit);
\end{lstlisting}

The chain is checked at every joint: \code{fold_case} must accept what
\code{lex} produces, and \code{emit} must accept what \code{fold_case}
produces. A mistake in the middle of the chain is a compile error naming
the stage involved, and the error is reported at the point where the
pipeline is written rather than at the point where the data happens to
flow.

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:pipeline-complete} builds two small DSLs on the feature.
The first converts temperatures; the second cleans up text. Both are
ordinary classes that inherit the feature tag and define their stages as
\code{static constexpr} members.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp, caption={Two DSLs built on the pipeline
feature. The units DSL converts Fahrenheit to Kelvin through two stages;
the transform DSL trims, lower-cases and shouts a string.},
label={lst:pipeline-complete}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Value types
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct Celsius
{
  double degrees;
};

struct Fahrenheit
{
  double degrees;
};

struct Kelvin
{
  double degrees;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A units DSL built on the Pipeline feature
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct UnitsDSL : dsl::DSL<UnitsDSL, dsl::Pipeline>
{
  static constexpr auto from_fahrenheit
      = dsl::pipe ([] (double f) { return Fahrenheit{ f }; });
  static constexpr auto to_celsius
      = dsl::pipe ([] (Fahrenheit f)
                   { return Celsius{ (f.degrees - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0 }; });
  static constexpr auto to_kelvin
      = dsl::pipe ([] (Celsius c) { return Kelvin{ c.degrees + 273.15 }; });
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A text-transform DSL built on the same feature
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct TransformDSL : dsl::DSL<TransformDSL, dsl::Pipeline>
{
  static constexpr auto trim
      = dsl::pipe ([] (std::string s)
                   {
                     auto b = s.find_first_not_of (" \t\n");
                     if (b == std::string::npos)
                       return std::string{};
                     auto e = s.find_last_not_of (" \t\n");
                     return s.substr (b, e - b + 1);
                   });
  static constexpr auto lower
      = dsl::pipe ([] (std::string s)
                   {
                     for (auto &ch : s)
                       ch = static_cast<char> (std::tolower (
                           static_cast<unsigned char> (ch)));
                     return s;
                   });
  static constexpr auto shout
      = dsl::pipe ([] (std::string s) { return s + "!"; });
};

int
main ()
{
  //  Three stages: the value flows from left to right.
  const auto kelvin
      = 212.0 | UnitsDSL::from_fahrenheit | UnitsDSL::to_celsius
      | UnitsDSL::to_kelvin;
  std::cout << "212F = " << kelvin.degrees << " K\n";

  //  A stage is taken by value, so it can be reused.
  const auto twice = dsl::pipe ([] (int x) { return x * 2; });
  std::cout << "3 | twice = " << (3 | twice) << '\n';
  std::cout << "4 | twice = " << (4 | twice) << '\n';

  //  The mixin contributes only the static identity function.
  auto shouty = TransformDSL::wrap ("  Hello, DSLtk  ") | TransformDSL::trim
                | TransformDSL::lower | TransformDSL::shout;
  std::cout << shouty << '\n';
}
\end{lstlisting}

The program prints

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
212F = 373.15 K
3 | twice = 6
4 | twice = 8
hello, dsltk!
\end{lstlisting}

Three observations follow from the listing. The first is that the stages
are \code{static constexpr} members of the DSL class, which means the
pipeline is a compile-time object graph: the stages are constructed once,
at compile time, and the pipeline itself is an expression that the compiler
can evaluate entirely at compile time if the stages are. The second is that
the pipeline is \emph{typed}: \code{to_celsius} accepts a
\code{Fahrenheit} and produces a \code{Celsius}, so a pipeline with the
stages in the wrong order does not compile. The third is that the value
types are distinct structs rather than a single \code{double} with a unit
tag, so a Fahrenheit cannot be passed where a Kelvin is expected. The
pipeline gives the reading; the distinct types give the safety.

