\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The right operand must be a stage.} The operator's second
parameter is \code{PipeStage<F>} by value, and \code{F} is deduced.
Consequently a bare lambda cannot be piped: \code{x | [] (int y) { return
y; }} does not compile, and the user must write \code{x | dsl::pipe(...
)}. This is deliberate. A raw lambda has no name, and the point of a stage
is to be a named, reusable, typed step.

\item \textbf{The left operand is forwarded, the right is copied.} The
value is passed as \code{T&&} and forwarded, so a stage may move from it.
The stage is copied, so it must be copy-constructible. A stage built from a
lambda that captures a move-only object therefore cannot be piped.

\item \textbf{Types must line up.} The facility performs no conversion
between stage input and stage output. If stage one produces a
\code{Celsius} and stage two expects a \code{Fahrenheit}, the pipeline does
not compile. Users who want implicit conversion must insert a stage that
converts, which is usually what they want in a DSL anyway.

\item \textbf{There is no variadic pipeline object.} The facility provides
an operator and a stage type, not a \code{Pipeline} class that can hold a
heterogeneous sequence of stages and be applied later. A user who wants
that must build it, typically by folding over a tuple of stages.

\item \textbf{\code{PipeStage} is not itself pipeable.} The operator
requires its right operand to be a \code{PipeStage}, and a
\code{PipeStage} is not one, so \code{x | stage | stage2} works but
\code{x | (stage | stage2)} does not --- the latter would require
\code{PipeStage} to be constructible from a \code{PipeStage}, and it is
not. Pipelines are written flat, which is the intended form.

\item \textbf{There is no error channel.} Every stage returns a value. A
stage that may fail must encode failure in its result type, for instance by
returning a \code{dsl::Result} or a \code{dsl::Maybe}; the pipeline
machinery has no opinion about it.

\item \textbf{Exception safety is the stage's own.} If a stage throws, the
exception propagates out of the pipeline unchanged. There is no
\emph{finally} stage, no resource guard, and no unwind-and-continue
behaviour.

\item \textbf{\code{wrap} adds nothing at run time.} It is the identity
function, and a good optimiser will erase it entirely. Its value is
syntactic: it lets a DSL class name the start of a pipeline.

\item \textbf{The D port spells the same idea differently.} In
\texttt{DSLtk.d}, \code{PipeStage} is defined at line 180 and the
application is expressed with \code{opBinaryRight}, so the D syntax is
\code{value | stage} as well, but the operator is a member of the
stage rather than a free function, and there is no \code{wrap} equivalent
with the same name.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The pipeline feature is the smallest of \DSLtk{}'s facilities and the one
with the widest reach. It contributes a stage type, a factory and one
overloaded operator; it needs no feature tag to work, it allocates nothing,
and it costs nothing beyond the work the stages do. Its whole contribution
is to change the reading order of a transformation chain from inside out to
top down.

The next chapter looks at a facility that composes conditions rather than
transformations. \chapref{ch:operators} introduces \api{dsl::Predicate},
the wrapper that turns a boolean-returning callable into a first-class
value that can be combined with \code{&}, \code{|} and \code{!}.
